course: dictionary
lesson: 6813
title: Orphan: a word with heavy meaning
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: An orphan is a child whose parents have both died. Orphan can be a noun («many famous characters are orphans») or a verb: to be orphaned means to become an orphan. Context helps you spot the meaning: «The Bible tells Christians to look after orphans and widows» shows that orphans need care and help from other people.
explain: Be careful with this word, because it can hurt. «A kid at school asks Aaliyah, "Where's your mum and dad?" "I live in a home." "What, like an orphan?" Aaliyah feels small.» Not every child in a home is an orphan. Most children in homes have parents who are alive but can't look after them right now. Always check the meaning: have both parents died?
explain: Orphan has other meanings too. An orphan animal has lost its mother, and «orphaned animals are raised in rescue centres». In printing and computing, an orphan is a single line left alone at the top or bottom of a page. Look at the words around the word to see which meaning fits.
fact: An orphan is a child whose parents have both died.
fact: To be orphaned means to become an orphan.
fact: Many famous story characters are orphans, such as Harry Potter, Anne of Green Gables and Oliver Twist.
fact: Most children in homes have parents who are alive but can't look after them right now, so they are not orphans.
fact: An orphan animal has lost its mother.
exercise: «Many famous characters are orphans, such as Harry Potter.» What is an orphan? | *A child whose parents have both died | A bird that cannot fly | A teacher who is very strict | A child who is always late for school
exercise: «Orphaned» means | To be very tall for your age | To win a prize at school | To go on a long holiday | *To become an orphan
exercise: «The orphaned fox cub was taken to a rescue centre.» What does orphaned mean here? | The cub had a new home in a town | *The cub had lost its mother | The cub could run very fast | The cub was very hungry
test: Is every child in a home an orphan? | No, because homes have no children | Yes, every child in a home is an orphan | Yes, but only if they are very young | *No, most have parents who are alive but can't look after them right now
test: In printing and computing, what is an orphan? | *A single line left alone at the top or bottom of a page | A word that is written in bold | A picture that has come loose from the text | A page with no numbers on it
exam: «Christians remember Jesus, and many faiths run charities for children without parents.» In this sentence, what does orphans most likely mean? | Children who are good at sport | *Children whose parents have died | Children who have just started school | A group of children who have a lot of money
